gw_in_vless.consent.a.body:
With a domain we recommend TLS with your own certificate: the server presents its real certificate, shows its own site and does not depend on other people’s sites. Reality makes the server pose as someone else’s site ({site}). This means:
- your server’s address does not belong to that site — the mismatch can be noticed from outside; if it is ever used for blocking, connections will be slowed down or dropped;
- if the site stops answering the server over TLS 1.3 or changes its settings, VLESS stops connecting until you pick another site (links have to be given out again)

gw_in_vless.consent.b.body:
With a domain we recommend TLS with your own certificate. Reality with your own site ({domain}) is the most careful kind of Reality: the address and the certificate match, no one else’s site is needed. What changes:
- the server shows the site and certificate of your domain, but VLESS itself moves off 443 to another port ({port}) — an unusual port next to a site stands out more than a single 443;
- the domain is still visible in the public certificate logs, as with TLS
